An aborted stream-json run's closing event now carries the partial reply. Before this, if a --output-format stream-json -p run was stopped by a signal, the reply text only appeared in the earlier messageUpdate deltas. The full gate is green: fmt, clippy with -D warnings, build, and every cargo test suite. The tree is clean and the commit has its full body.

What I found first (local stub only, posted on #1228 before any code edit): the deltas streamed NEW ANSWER, but the agentEnd aborted message had "". Reading yoagent 0.27.9's source explains it. When a run is cancelled, it builds the closing message with an empty text block, and the cancel error carries no partial text, so the text is dropped upstream. I filed #1237 (label upstream) asking yoagent to keep it.

What changed:
• New `src/prompt/stream_aborted_text.rs`: it collects the current message's text deltas and starts over at every messageStart, so a retried attempt's text is thrown away. It fills only an assistant message that is aborted and has no text, in its messageEnd and in agentEnd. It uses the existing text field; there is no new event type. The saved session (agent.messages()) still holds the empty message; only #1237 can fix that.
• Unit tests (5): an aborted empty message gets the partial; a normal end is byte-identical; an aborted message that already has text is byte-identical; a retry drops the first attempt's text; with nothing streamed the message stays empty.
• `src/prompt.rs`: three lines of wiring, so it goes from 3993 to 3996 lines. I updated its limit in tests/module_size.rs with the reason.
• Binary tests: the SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGINT tests in tests/output_failed_run.rs now also check that the agentEnd text equals the streamed deltas.
• Recall control (one command): I disabled the fill (marker counted 1), and the SIGTERM test failed by name with "" against "NEW ANSWER". After restoring it (marker 0) the test passed, and git status showed only this task's files.
• Docs: docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md now says what the closing event carries.

The other case #1228 mentioned (a run that doesn't stop within about 2 s ends with no agentEnd at all): I didn't test it. No local stub can make yoyo's built-in providers ignore the cancel, because the Anthropic provider checks for it on every stream read. It could still happen some other way, for example a tool still running when the signal lands. It's tracked in a new issue, #1238, and the docs line now points there.

The before-row, after-row and control result are all posted on #1228, which closes when this is pushed.
